      "thm:collision-uniform-root-n",
      "lem:uniform-summary-concentration"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Choose \\(C_0\\) in \\(r_{n,\\alpha}\\) as in \\(\\mathrm{lem:uniform\\text{-}summary\\text{-}concentration}\\).  Uniformly in \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\),\n\\[\nQ_P^{(n)}(E_P)\n=Q_P^{(n)}\\{d_S(\\widehat S_n,S(P))\\le r_{n,\\alpha}\\}\n\\ge1-\\alpha.                                                        \\tag{107}\n\\]\nOn \\(E_P\\), nearest-point optimality gives\n\\[\nd_S\\{S(P),\\Pi(\\widehat S_n)\\}\\le2r_{n,\\alpha}.                      \\tag{108}\n\\]\nSince \\(S(P)\\in\\mathcal K\\) and \\(\\overline F(S(P))=\\nu_P\\), (108) makes \\(S(P)\\) feasible in the theoretical summary-inversion definition.  Hence \\(\\nu_P\\in\\mathcal C_{n,\\alpha}\\).\n\nThe deterministic lattice bound gives on the same event\n\\[\nW_1(\\widehat\\lambda_n,\\nu_P)\n\\le C_{\\mathrm{lat}}(r_{n,\\alpha}+n^{-1/2})\n=R_{n,\\alpha}.                                                       \\tag{109}\n\\]\nLatent-arm positivity implies \\(p_u\\ge2\\pi_0\\); hence every distinct atom of \\(\\nu_P\\), after aggregation of equal effects, has mass at least \\(2\\pi_0\\ge m_\\star\\).  Thus \\(\\nu_P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\le k,m_\\star}([-L_{\\tau},L_{\\tau}])\\), and (109) proves \\(\\nu_P\\in\\mathcal C^{\\mathrm{alg}}_{n,\\alpha}\\).  Together with (107), this proves simultaneous honest coverage.\n\nFor any two summaries \\(q,q'\\) feasible for the theoretical set,\n\\[\nd_S(q,q')\\le4r_{n,\\alpha}.\n\\]\nThe gap-free modulus therefore yields\n\\(W_1(\\overline F(q),\\overline F(q'))\\le4C_{\\mathrm{mod}}r_{n,\\alpha}\\).  For any \\(\\xi,\\xi'\\in\\mathcal C^{\\mathrm{alg}}_{n,\\alpha}\\),\n\\[\nW_1(\\xi,\\xi')\n\\le W_1(\\xi,\\widehat\\lambda_n)+W_1(\\widehat\\lambda_n,\\xi')\n\\le2R_{n,\\alpha}.                                                    \\tag{110}\n\\]\nThe theoretical set contains \\(\\widehat\\nu_n\\) whenever \\(\\mathcal K\\ne\\varnothing\\), and equals \\(\\{\\delta_0\\}\\) otherwise, by \\(\\mathrm{prop:summary\\text{-}repair\\text{-}total\\text{-}borel}\\).  The algorithmic set contains \\(\\widehat\\lambda_n\\), because the lattice proposition gives its atom floor and its distance from itself is zero.  Thus both sets are nonempty on every sample.\n\nFinally, write the center and candidate in ordered atomic form.  A nonnegative array \\((\\gamma_{a\\ell})\\) with the displayed row and column sums is precisely a coupling between them, and its displayed objective is the transport cost.  Minimizing that cost over such arrays is the definition of \\(W_1\\) for finite atomic laws.  Enumeration of \\(1\\le s\\le k\\) therefore gives exactly the asserted fixed-dimensional constrained representation.  No step relates the two confidence sets by equality or inclusion.",
    "justification": "The theorem simultaneously covers the retained theoretical image and an evaluable atom-floor outer set, with root-\\(n\\) Wasserstein diameters.",
    "gap": "The two honest sets serve different purposes and are not claimed equal or nested.",
    "consumer": "The outer set supports sample-evaluable aggregate latent-effect-law reporting without preliminary separation.",
